# Bloom, halation, starbursts and anamorphic streaks -- one algorithm.
#
# The core is Tim's description and it is exactly right: threshold the
# highlights, blur them, add them back. What makes the rest fall out of the same
# code is that halation and diffraction spikes are the SAME three steps with a
# different spread. Only the kernel changes.
#
# ONE STAGE RATHER THAN FOUR, deliberately. Every one of these thresholds the
# same highlights and adds into the same image, so as separate stages each would
# re-threshold and re-add -- two of them together would count the highlights
# twice, and the second would bloom the first one's glow. Here the threshold
# happens once and the spreads are summed before the merge.
#
# WHAT THE PARAMETERS MEAN, because they follow from the optics rather than from
# a list of looks:
#
#   threshold   how bright a pixel must be to glow. On a scene-linear image with
#               real headroom this usefully goes ABOVE 1 -- the Bay Bridge test
#               shot peaks at 8.4, so a threshold of 1.0 catches only the lamps
#               and leaves the sky alone.
#   knee        the soft band below the threshold. A hard cutoff leaves a
#               visible contour wherever a gradient crosses it; the knee is what
#               makes the glow read as light rather than as a mask.
#   spread      blur sigma, in pixels. The visible glow reaches about 2.5x this.
#   intensity   how much is added back. 0 turns the stage off entirely and the
#               pipeline skips it.
#
#   dispersion  HALATION. Not a tint -- a spread that differs per channel.
#               Halation is light passing through the emulsion, reflecting off
#               the backing, and re-exposing from below; red penetrates deepest,
#               so red is what comes back. At dispersion 1 red spreads furthest
#               and blue least, which puts a warm fringe at the EDGE of a
#               highlight rather than colouring the whole glow.
#
#   streak      diffraction spikes from the aperture blades.
#   blades      an EVEN count gives that many spikes, an ODD count gives twice
#               as many -- an odd polygon has no parallel edges, so each
#               diffracts into its own spike. Six blades, six spikes; five
#               blades, ten. Set blades = 1 for the two-spike anamorphic look.
#   streak_angle  rotates the pattern. 0 is horizontal.
#
# A NOTE ON ENERGY, because the two spreads deliberately differ. The round blur
# NORMALISES: it redistributes light, so a small bright source spread over 20x
# the area gets 20x dimmer, which is what scattering does. A diffraction spike
# does not -- the light in the spike is the part of the wavefront the aperture
# edge bent, arriving IN ADDITION to what went straight through. Normalising it
# over the length of the line divided a point source by about a hundred and made
# the spikes invisible.
#
# Try it on a night shot with point lights. A daylight landscape has nothing
# bright enough to bloom without dropping the threshold so far that the whole
# frame glows.

src = image("test")

threshold = slider("threshold", 0, 4, 1.0)
spread    = slider("spread (px)", 1, 120, 20)
intensity = slider("intensity", 0, 3, 1.0)

# Halation: red spreads furthest. 0 is achromatic bloom.
dispersion = slider("dispersion (halation)", 0, 1, 0)

# Diffraction. streak 0 is none; blades 1 gives the two-spike anamorphic look.
streak = slider("streak", 0, 2, 0)
blades = slider("aperture blades", 1, 16, 6)
angle  = slider("streak angle", 0, 180, 0)

src => bloom(threshold = threshold,
             spread = spread,
             intensity = intensity,
             dispersion = dispersion,
             streak = streak,
             blades = blades,
             streak_angle = angle)
    => tonemap()
    => display("bloom")
